23:31:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. You're not “making it up,” and I'm not dismissing what you learned. That first-in-first-out (FIFO) model is indeed taught in many basic nutrition and health classes, and for good reason: it's a useful, simple approximation that works in ordinary circumstances. But there's a big difference between a teaching simplification and the actual physiological mechanism. Let me honor what you were taught and then show where it fits with what I've been saying. **Why FIFO is taught in health class** For someone who eats three meals a day, chewing normally, the digestion of a typical meal goes something like this: - You eat lunch at noon. Over the next 1–2 hours it sits in the stomach, gets churned, and turns into liquid chyme that gradually squirts into the small intestine. - By about 2–3 p.m., most of that meal has left the stomach. - If you then eat a snack at 3 p.m., the stomach still contains very little of the previous meal, mainly just a bit of lingering residue. The new snack becomes the primary contents, and the process repeats. Under this pattern, it *looks* like the stomach empties in the order things were eaten, because each meal is largely gone before the next one arrives. You rarely have two completely distinct layers of food sitting in the stomach at the same time. So FIFO is a reasonable rule of thumb that helps students grasp the concept without getting lost in antral sieving, retropulsion, and rheology. It's a *pedagogical shortcut*, not a biological law. **The reason the shortcut sometimes breaks** The question you originally asked—eating every ~30 minutes—is exactly the scenario where the simplistic FIFO model collapses, because now the stomach never gets a chance to fully empty between eating events. You end up with a continuous, layered load. Health class doesn't usually cover that edge case; it teaches you what you need to understand normal meal spacing. But when you eat that frequently, you need the more detailed explanation: the stomach uses a physical sieve (the pylorus) that discriminates based on particle size and liquidity, not temporal order. In typical meals, older food happens to be more broken down, so it leaves first, reinforcing the FIFO illusion. But the mechanism is consistency, not chronology. **Reconciling the two:** - Your teacher was giving you a *model* that works for 90% of daily life. It's not "wrong" in that context; it's just incomplete. - What I've been describing is the *mechanism* that explains why that model holds most of the time and, crucially, why it fails under continuous eating or unusual food properties. Both views can coexist: FIFO is an emergent pattern, not a built-in rule. So you didn't make it up, your teacher didn't lie, and the body's exquisite, sieve-like behavior doesn't negate the basic health class story—it just underlies it. The next time you eat leftovers with fresh food, you can now explain to your teacher why the leftovers leave first: because they're softer, not because they're older. That's some advanced health class thinking right there.
